       2/       0 :                     ; VDP DMA fill + data-port-read experiment ROM (clean-room, authored here).
       3/       0 :                     ;
       4/       0 :                     ; Two cells for the DMA + FIFO push (VDP recon R4(b) / R1 open remainders),
       5/       0 :                     ; driven by BlastEm's GDB-remote stub exactly like vdp_pending.asm.
       6/       0 :                     ;
       7/       0 :                     ; Cell 0 (VRAM fill baseline, R4(b)): program a VRAM fill DMA of 8 bytes at
       8/       0 :                     ;   $0400 with fill value $EE (data word $EEEE -> top byte for VRAM), then read
       9/       0 :                     ;   VRAM $0400/$0402 back. Confirms the fill writes the top byte to consecutive
      10/       0 :                     ;   addresses (autoincrement 1) and that DMA-enable (reg1 bit4) gates it. This
      11/       0 :                     ;   is the concrete behavior slice F implements — pinned against hardware here.
      12/       0 :                     ;
      13/       0 :                     ; Cell 1 (data-port READ while a WRITE command is armed, R1 open cell): arm the
      14/       0 :                     ;   FIRST word of a VRAM-WRITE command @ $0200, then perform a data-port READ.
      15/       0 :                     ;   Recon R1 pins this configuration as a hardware LOCKUP ("setup a write then
      16/       0 :                     ;   read -> the 68K hangs"). BlastEm's response (hang = watchdog timeout, or a
      17/       0 :                     ;   returned value) is recorded by the driver — a low-exposure cell the recon
      18/       0 :                     ;   flags as bracketed by the lockup; we record honestly, do not force a pin.
      19/       0 :                     ;
      20/       0 :                     ; Control block (driver writes $FF9000.b before `c`):
      21/       0 :                     ;   0 -> cell 0 (VRAM fill baseline)
      22/       0 :                     ;   1 -> cell 1 (data-read while write-armed; may hang = lockup)
      23/       0 :                     ;
      24/       0 :                     ; Observable RAM:
      25/       0 :                     ;   $FF8000.w VRAM[$0400]  (fill readback; expect $EEEE for cell 0)
      26/       0 :                     ;   $FF8002.w VRAM[$0402]  (fill readback; expect $EEEE for cell 0)
      27/       0 :                     ;   $FF8004.w VRAM[$0406]  (fill tail; expect $EEEE if all 8 bytes filled)
      28/       0 :                     ;   $FF8008.w cell-1 data-read value (if it did not hang)
      29/       0 :                     ;   $FF8010.w done marker $C0DE  (absent => the machine hung, i.e. lockup)
